8795a3f5dd140e9", "kind": "experiment", "role": "T1", "tree_id": "e1:T1", "mission_id": "a8795a3f5dd140e9", "facts": {"commit": "6f2654ac59cb6fcc13b6d506feefd5e6ae26a559", "base_commit": "22138f39a9036399442f78956289de1c33a07b3d", "parent_id": null, "source_ids": [], "source_count": 0}, "measured_outcome": {"status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.0011331403584821822, "score": 0.6201968205488412}, "diagnostic": "", "model_interpretation": {"claim": "Cooperatively stage each 256-source tile's x, y, z, and mass values in 4 KiB of static shared memory so the block's output threads reuse them instead of repeatedly fetching those source fields from global memory. The sequential pairwise arithmetic and one-output-per-active-thread strategy remain close to baseline; barriers and shared-memory occupancy costs may offset the reduced global loads, so this is a performance hypothesis for measurement.", "transformations": "{"files":["src/nbody_kernel.cu"],"retained":["Stage source x, y, z, and mass in four 256-element shared-memory arrays, loading each tile cooperatively.","Synchronize after tile loads and after tile consumption so all output lanes, including tail-block lanes, safely participate before shared storage is reused.","Keep the source-particle accumulation in ascending tile/source order and guard output work and writes with i < n."]}", "applicability": "", "next_experiment": "", "evidence_ids": []}, "source_available": true, "details_are_bounded": true}, {"id": "fcfebc51a67f48d7", "kind": "experiment", "role": "T2", "tree_id": "e1:T2", "mission_id": "fcfebc51a67f48d7", "facts": {"commit": "f63c9b81df70aac6a75035827434cbdb26a3afac", "base_commit": "22138f39a9036399442f78956289de1c33a07b3d", "parent_id": null, "source_ids": [], "source_count": 0}, "measured_outcome": {"status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.002745536300643952, "score": 0.40261046959044094}, "diagnostic": "", "model_interpretation": {"claim": "Giving each thread two outputs lets it load each source particle's position and mass once and reuse those values for both targets. Mapping the targets to threadIdx.x and threadIdx.x + blockDim.x preserves contiguous lane accesses for each output group. This may reduce repeated source loads, while the additional target state and accumulators may increase register pressure; official measurements are needed to assess the tradeoff.", "transformations": "{"files":["src/launch.json","src/nbody_kernel.cu"],"retained":["Assign two output particles per thread, with the second target at first + blockDim.x so each target group remains contiguous across lanes.","Stream each source particle's position and mass once per thread and reuse the values for both target force calculations.","Maintain separate target states and force accumulators; guard the second target for partial groups and write each valid output once.","Set items_per_block to 512 while retaining block=256 and shared_memory_bytes=0."]}", "applicability": "", "next_experiment": "", "evidence_ids": []}, "source_available": true, "details_are_bounded": true}, {"id": "253c00e28c904a54", "kind": "experiment", "role": "T3", "tree_id": "e1:T3", "mission_id": "253c00e28c904a54", "facts": {"commit": "7148961870199c9bcce2304b698d6b995bada423", "base_commit": "22138f39a9036399442f78956289de1c33a07b3d", "parent_id": null, "source_ids": [], "source_count": 0}, "measured_outcome": {"status": "incorrect", "correct": false, "mean_seconds": null, "score": 0.0}, "diagnostic": "Protected correctness checks failed", "model_interpretation": {"claim": "With the same per-thread mapping, global-memory accesses, rsqrtf, and target-mass cancellation, forming (G * neighbor_mass * invDist) and (invDist * invDist) independently may shorten the dependent multiplication chain for each pair. This is a performance hypothesis; the changed float32 rounding must pass the independent oracle.", "transformations": "{"files":["src/nbody_kernel.cu"],"retained":["Keep one output per thread, the pairwise global-memory strategy, explicit rsqrtf, G=1, softening, update order, and the self-interaction skip.","Reorder the reciprocal-cube multiplications so the neighbor-mass/invDist product and invDist-squared product can be formed independently before their final multiplication."]}", "applicability": "", "next_experiment": "", "evidence_ids": []}, "source_available": true, "details_are_bounded": true}, {"id": "42ddd5d53dc94097", "kind": "experiment", "role": "T4", "tree_id": "e1:T4", "mission_id": "42ddd5d53dc94097", "facts": {"commit": "08105ca24041a47613878a4745fe1065b1fe0007", "base_commit": "22138f39a9036399442f78956289de1c33a07b3d", "parent_id": null, "source_ids": [], "source_count": 0}, "measured_outcome": {"status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.001851955682455682, "score": 0.4997835901596435}, "diagnostic": "", "model_interpretation": {"claim": "Avec block=256 et items_per_block=128, la grille lance au moins N threads et l’indice global linéaire attribue chaque sortie valide à un seul thread; le garde traite les tails et les threads excédentaires. Cette géométrie dépareillée ajoute des threads inactifs, donc seule la mesure de la moyenne géométrique peut dire si elle est utile.", "transformations": "{"files":["src/launch.json","src/nbody_kernel.cu"],"retained":["Configurer block=256, items_per_block=128 et shared_memory_bytes=0; le choix block >= items_per_block garantit assez de threads avec grid.x=ceil(N/items_per_block).","Utiliser l’indice global linéaire non signé blockIdx.x*blockDim.x+threadIdx.x, puis retourner si i>=n: les indices 0..N-1 sont chacun traités une fois et les threads de queue n’écrivent rien."]}", "applicability": "", "next_experiment": "", "evidence_ids": []}, "source_available": true, "details_are_bounded": true}, {"id": "baseline-e1", "kind": "experiment", "role": "baseline", "tree_id": "baseline", "mission_id": "baseline-e1", "facts": {"commit": "22138f39a9036399442f78956289de1c33a07b3d", "base_commit": "22138f39a9036399442f78956289de1c33a07b3d", "parent_id": null, "source_ids": [], "source_count": 0}, "measured_outcome": {"status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.0018503532502855123, "score": 0.5}, "diagnostic": "", "model_interpretation": {"claim": "", "transformations": "{}", "applicability": "", "next_experiment": "", "evidence_ids": []}, "source_available": true, "details_are_bounded": true}], "offset": 0, "total": 14, "next_offset": 6}, "evidence_ids": ["a8472c694eaa4bfe", "a8795a3f5dd140e9", "fcfebc51a67f48d7", "253c00e28c904a54", "42ddd5d53dc94097", "baseline-e1"], "remaining_reads": 3, "max_result_characters": 12000, "provenance": "controller facts and separately labelled model interpretations", "current_base_commit": "071734546436a268a25633a36ef99f97e50219b2", "selected_parent_id": null, "reference": {"id": "a8472c694eaa4bfe", "kind": "experiment", "role": "G", "tree_id": "e1:G-merge-1-1", "mission_id": "a8472c694eaa4bfe", "facts": {"commit": "071734546436a268a25633a36ef99f97e50219b2", "base_commit": "6f2654ac59cb6fcc13b6d506feefd5e6ae26a559", "parent_id": null, "source_ids": ["a8795a3f5dd140e9"], "source_count": 1}, "measured_outcome": {"status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.0011249001639228504, "score": 0.6219145036349258}, "diagnostic": "", "model_interpretation": {"claim": "Increasing the shared source tile from 256 to 512 may halve tile-loop and barrier iterations while remaining within the shared-memory limit; the larger shared allocation and longer inner loop may offset that benefit. This is an unmeasured hypothesis, not a claimed speedup.", "transformations": "{"files":["src/nbody_kernel.cu"],"retained":["Retain T1's cooperative staging of source positions and masses in shared memory.","Increase the source tile size from 256 to 512; leave the kernel interface, pairwise arithmetic, output mapping, and launch configuration unchanged."]}", "applicability": "", "next_experiment": "", "evidence_ids": []}, "source_available": true, "details_are_bounded": true}, "catalog": {"schema": "hpc-forest-memory-v1", "items": [{"role": "T1", "subject": "Shared-memory tiling for source-particle reuse", "objective": "Explore cooperative staging of source-particle positions and masses into shared memory so each source tile is reused by multiple output particles. Focus on tile loading, synchronization, tail handling, and shared-memory/occupancy tradeoffs; keep the pairwise arithmetic and output strategy otherwise close to the baseline, and retain the default launch geometry unless the tile requires a change. Respect the 49152-byte shared-memory limit and ensure every output is written exactly once for every N. Evaluate only programs that pass the full correctness suite and compare the official geometric-mean timing across N=4096, 8192, and 16384 under the prescribed search protocol.", "records": 2, "text_truncated": false}, {"role": "T2", "subject": "Register-blocked multiple outputs per thread", "objective": "Explore register blocking by assigning each thread a small group of output particles and maintaining separate target states and force accumulators while streaming source particles. Do not add shared-memory source tiling or change the force formula, so this tests a distinct reuse strategy. Experiment with mappings that preserve efficient input/output access and control register pressure; handle partial groups and all legal N so every output is produced exactly once. Record correctness, configuration, and official geometric-mean timings under the prescribed search protocol.", "records": 2, "text_truncated": false}, {"role": "T3", "subject": "Pairwise arithmetic and numerical-accuracy tradeoffs", "objective": "Explore pairwise arithmetic changes while keeping the baseline one-output-per-thread mapping and global-memory strategy as close as practical. Measure explicit rsqrtf-based formulations and the algebraic cancellation of target mass when computing acceleration; optionally test eliminating the self-interaction branch only when the softened self term remains well-defined. Do not enable global fast math or specialize to benchmark inputs. Preserve G=1, softening=0.001f, update order, exact masses, and required float32 behavior; reject any candidate that fails the independent oracle or any required case, including coincident positions, extreme isolated mass, and zero or negative dt.", "records": 4, "text_truncated": false}, {"role": "T4", "subject": "Launch geometry and output-index mapping", "objective": "Explore legal block and items_per_block configurations and the corresponding output-index mapping using a baseline-style one-output-per-thread kernel, without shared-memory tiling or register blocking. Ensure the mapping honors grid.x=ceil(N/items_per_block), covers every output exactly once even when the two values differ, and handles tails for all N. Keep shared_memory_bytes zero unless required by the unchanged kernel. Compare correct candidates by the prescribed geometric-mean kernel time, not by a single size.", "records": 2, "text_truncated": false}], "offset": 0, "total": 4, "next_offset": null}, "last_read": {"schema": "hpc-forest-memory-v1", "id": "fcfebc51a67f48d7", "commit": "f63c9b81df70aac6a75035827434cbdb26a3afac", "snapshot_digest": "53c883f67b954fbfcdf8f67cad48e39d115964df20bc7ffb0ea99c0ec45d85ba", "offset_unit": "characters", "path": "src/nbody_kernel.cu", "file_sha256": "ffd6002a087a2f27985bb00013677b41fb1a4c57b392220ab7d950803ced1ac5", "total_characters": 2399, "text": "struct Particle {
    float x, y, z;
    float vx, vy, vz;
    float mass;
};

extern "C" __global__ void nbody_step_naive(const Particle* in, Particle* out, int n, float dt) {
    const unsigned int first = blockIdx.x * (2u * blockDim.x) + threadIdx.x;
    const unsigned int limit = static_cast<unsigned int>(n);
    if (first >= limit) return;

    const unsigned int second = first + blockDim.x;
    const bool has_second = second < limit;

    const float G = 1.0f;
    const float softening = 0.001f;

    Particle p0 = in[first];
    Particle p1;
    if (has_second) p1 = in[second];

    float fx0 = 0.0f;
    float fy0 = 0.0f;
    float fz0 = 0.0f;
    float fx1 = 0.0f;
    float fy1 = 0.0f;
    float fz1 = 0.0f;

    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        const float source_x = in[j].x;
        const float source_y = in[j].y;
        const float source_z = in[j].z;
        const float source_mass = in[j].mass;
        const unsigned int source_index = static_cast<unsigned int>(j);

        if (first != source_index) {
            const float dx = source_x - p0.x;
            const float dy = source_y - p0.y;
            const float dz = source_z - p0.z;
            const float distSq = dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz + softening;
            const float dist = sqrtf(distSq);
            const float f = (G * p0.mass * source_mass) / (distSq * dist);
            fx0 += f * dx;
            fy0 += f * dy;
            fz0 += f * dz;
        }

        if (has_second && second != source_index) {
            const float dx = source_x - p1.x;
            const float dy = source_y - p1.y;
            const float dz = source_z - p1.z;
            const float distSq = dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz + softening;
            const float dist = sqrtf(distSq);
            const float f = (G * p1.mass * source_mass) / (distSq * dist);
            fx1 += f * dx;
            fy1 += f * dy;
            fz1 += f * dz;
        }
    }

    p0.vx += (fx0 / p0.mass) * dt;
    p0.vy += (fy0 / p0.mass) * dt;
    p0.vz += (fz0 / p0.mass) * dt;
    p0.x += p0.vx * dt;
    p0.y += p0.vy * dt;
    p0.z += p0.vz * dt;
    out[first] = p0;

    if (has_second) {
        p1.vx += (fx1 / p1.mass) * dt;
        p1.vy += (fy1 / p1.mass) * dt;
        p1.vz += (fz1 / p1.mass) * dt;
        p1.x += p1.vx * dt;
        p1.y += p1.vy * dt;
        p1.z += p1.vz * dt;
        out[second] = p1;
    }
}
